6. Approved Business Rules
ID	Business rule
BR-01	At least one contact method (email or phone) is required at registration.
BR-02	An email address must be unique when provided.
BR-03	A phone number must be unique when provided.
BR-04	Public registration always assigns CUSTOMER role on the server; the client cannot select a privileged role.
BR-05	ADMIN accounts can only be provisioned through an authorized, protected administrative workflow.
BR-06	A customer may log in before contact verification, subject to account status and valid credentials.
BR-07	An unverified customer may browse products and add products to the cart.
BR-08	At least one contact method supplied by the customer must be verified before checkout.
BR-09	If both email and phone are supplied, verifying either one is sufficient for the initial checkout policy.
BR-10	Suspended or locked accounts cannot authenticate.
BR-11	A customer may access and modify only their own profile and addresses, except separately authorized admin operations.
BR-12	Passwords must never be stored in plaintext; an adaptive password-hashing algorithm must be used.
BR-13	Account status and contact-verification status are separate concepts and must be modeled independently.

7. Account and Verification Model
Account lifecycle and contact verification must be modeled separately. For example, an account can be PENDING_VERIFICATION while emailVerified and phoneVerified independently indicate which contact methods have been verified.
Account status	Meaning
PENDING_VERIFICATION	Account exists but no required contact verification has yet been completed.
ACTIVE	Account is eligible for normal use, subject to authorization and other policies.
SUSPENDED	Account access is administratively restricted.
LOCKED	Account authentication is temporarily blocked due to a security policy or repeated failures.

7.1 Checkout eligibility
Checkout is permitted only when the account is otherwise eligible and at least one contact method provided by the customer is verified. If the customer provided only an email, that email must be verified. If only a phone number was provided, that phone number must be verified. If both were provided, either one satisfies the initial policy.
Cart Service owns cart operations. The checkout/order workflow must enforce this policy by obtaining trusted verification information from User Service through an approved API or integration contract. It must not directly read the User Service database.
8. Non-Functional Requirements
Quality attribute	Requirement
Security	Secure password hashing, authorization checks, token lifecycle controls, input validation, and externalized secrets.
Performance	Indexed lookups for normalized email and phone identifiers; measurable latency targets will be defined during deployment planning.
Scalability	Service boundaries and stateless request handling should support horizontal scaling.
Reliability	Database and verification-provider failures must not result in contacts being falsely marked verified.
Maintainability	Clear layering, SOLID principles, automated tests, and database migrations.
Observability	Structured logs, correlation IDs, health checks, and security events without logging secrets.
Cost control	Use free/open-source technologies and local integrations initially; paid providers are optional future integrations.

